    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.ai\r\n\r\n### Description\r\n\r\nUsing either the `i` or `a` textobjects in the cmdline window (`q:`, `q/`, `q?`) will abruptly close said window. I believe the cause is in [this function](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/0e82cef370ffd355815a5159307c7b74382ff657/lua/mini/ai.lua#L1908), in particular the `<C-\\><C-n>` sequence. Perhaps `<Esc>` or `<C-c>` would suffice?\r\n\r\n### Neovim version\r\n\r\nv0.9.0-dev-883+g1391385ba\r\n\r\n### Steps to reproduce\r\n\r\n1. Open the command line window via `q:`.\r\n2. Insert `(hello)` into an open line.\r\n3. Position cursor inside the parenthesis. Use `vi(` in normal mode.\r\n\r\nNote that this can be reproduced with any built in textobject. If performing a change (EG: `ci(`), nvim will enter insert mode, but will exit the cmdline window as soon as the user returns to normal mode.\r\n\r\n### Expected behavior\r\n\r\nThe cmdline window shouldn't close after using `mini.ai` textobject.\r\n\r\n### Actual behavior\r\n\r\nThe cmdline window abruptly closes.",
